    92	## The N/2 Universality
    93	
    94	Five independent measures converge on the same threshold:
    95	
    96	| Measure | Value at boundary | Interpretation |
    97	|---------|------------------|----------------|
    98	| Approximate degree | N/2 | Polynomial needs degree >= N/2 to approximate |
    99	| Communication rank | 2^{N/2-1} + 2 | Communication protocol needs N/2 bits |
    100	| PTF degree | N/2 | Threshold polynomial needs degree N/2 |
    101	| LFSR complexity | N/2 | Linear recurrence needs length N/2 |
    102	| Per-bit R-correlation crossover | bit N/2 | Smooth approximation accurate above bit N/2 |
    103	
    104	This universality suggests a fundamental information-theoretic divide:
    105	the top N/2 bits of p(n) are determined by the smooth part R^{-1}(n)
    106	(computable in O(polylog) time), while the bottom N/2 bits encode
    107	oscillatory contributions from ~10^48 zeta zeros with GUE-random phases.
